# A flasher: a 555 timer running free, an astable, blinking an LED about once a second.
#
# C1 charges through R1 and R2 until it reaches two thirds of the supply, 6 V, where the timer's
# threshold resets it: its output falls, and its discharge pin, between R1 and R2, is switched to
# ground, so C1 empties through R2 alone. At a third of the supply, 3 V, the trigger sets it again,
# and the discharge lets go. Each half is C1 crossing between 3 V and 6 V, ln 2 of a time constant:
# high for 0.693 (R1 + R2) C1, 0.54 s, and low for 0.693 R2 C1, 0.47 s, a period of a second, the
# datasheet's 1.44 / ((R1 + 2 R2) C1). The first high is longer, C1 charging from empty.
#
# The output drives D1 through R3 while it is high. C2 keeps noise off the control pin, which sets
# both levels. The circuit runs in real time.

speed 1

battery  B1   0 2  0 14  9V
wire          0 2  13 2
timer    U1   12 8  NE555
wire          13 5  13 2
wire          11 5  11 4
wire          11 4  13 4
resistor R1   6 2  6 7  10k
wire          6 7  9 7
resistor R2   6 7  6 11  68k
wire          9 8  9 11
wire          6 11  9 11
capacitor C1  6 11  6 14  10u
wire          11 11  11 14
capacitor C2  13 11  13 14  10n
resistor R3   15 8  18 8  470
led      D1   18 8  18 12  red
wire          18 12  18 14
wire          0 14  18 14
ground        0 14
